## Keep a symptom diary

Write down your **symptoms**, what you **ate and drank**, what you were **doing**, the **temperature**, your **stress levels** and, if relevant, where you are in your **period**. Over a few weeks, patterns can show your triggers. Take the diary to your appointments.

## Food

Some people find certain foods make their symptoms worse. Do **not** cut out lots of foods on your own, as this can make it hard to eat a healthy diet. If you think food is a trigger, ask your GP or specialist about seeing a **dietitian**.

## Heat and temperature

- avoid **very hot baths and showers**
- dress in **layers**, so you can adjust easily
- use a **fan** and keep cool in hot weather
- avoid sudden changes in temperature where you can

## Stress, rest and exercise

- **pace yourself** and plan rest, especially on busy days
- try gentle ways to manage stress, such as breathing exercises
- if exercise is a trigger, start **slowly** and build up gently, and stop if you feel unwell

## Plan for flares

- keep your **medicines** with you, and a **written list** of what you take
- agree a **flare plan** with your doctor, so you know what to take and when to get help
- tell people you live, work or study with what to do

## Be ready for anaphylaxis

If you have **adrenaline auto-injectors**:

- carry **2 with you at all times**
- check the **expiry dates** and sign up for reminders from the maker
- consider a **medical ID** bracelet or card

{% callout type="danger" title="In an emergency" %}
If you have signs of anaphylaxis, use your adrenaline auto-injector straight away, then call **999** and say **"anaphylaxis"**. Lie down with your legs raised, or sit up if you are struggling to breathe. Use your second auto-injector after 5 minutes if you are not getting better.
{% /callout %}

## Your mental health

Living with symptoms that are hard to predict, and that others may not understand, can be very stressful. Talk to your GP if you are feeling low or anxious. You can call **Samaritans** free, any time, on **116 123**.

## Support

**Mast Cell Action** is a UK charity that offers information and support for people with MCAS and their families.
